====================================================================================================
S2. Where the kernels differ -- binned in g_obs, which is Upsilon-FREE
====================================================================================================
  A FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS BINNED IN g_bar AND THAT WAS WRONG, so it is worth stating why.
  g_bar depends on Upsilon, and each kernel here carries its OWN best-fit Upsilon, so g_bar
  bins hold DIFFERENT POINTS for different kernels and the comparison is confounded: the
  apparent spread then mixes a genuine tail difference with a bin-membership difference. It
  reported 0.064 dex in the top bin, which is 1.9x sigma_int and would have looked like real
  leverage. Binning instead in g_obs = V_obs^2/R -- pure observation, no Upsilon -- fixes it:
  every kernel is now scored on exactly the same points in every bin.
  AND THE g_obs BINNING ALONE IS STILL NOT ENOUGH, which is the substantive finding of this
  section. With each kernel at its OWN best Upsilon the top bin still separates by ~0.05 dex.
  That is not tail leverage either: it is the Upsilon DEGENERACY, each kernel absorbing its
  shape difference into a different Upsilon (0.62 to 0.76 here). Holding Upsilon SHARED
  isolates pure kernel shape, and the top-bin spread then collapses and its ORDERING FLIPS
  with Upsilon -- the signature of a degeneracy rather than a measurement. All three panels
  are shown because only the third answers the question asked.

