  [OK] at SHARED Upsilon the highest-acceleration bin separates the kernels by at most 0.0189 dex across Upsilon = 0.5-0.9 (< 0.034 dex) -- so there is no bin, including the one nearest the planets, where SPARC prefers a tail exponent

  WHERE THE REAL DISCRIMINATING POWER SITS. At shared Upsilon the LARGEST spreads
  (0.0730 dex, above sigma_int) are in the TRANSITION bins around a0, not in
  the tail. That is the expected place for interpolations to differ, and it is also where each
  kernel's own Upsilon is fitted to remove the difference -- which is why the global numbers in
  S1 agree to 0.008 dex. The tail itself contributes essentially nothing either way.

====================================================================================================
S3. Bulge galaxies only -- the subsample the Cassini literature says carries the trade-off
====================================================================================================
  Desmond+2024 and Park+2026 both report their Q2 significance COLLAPSING when the
  bulge-dominated galaxies are cut (8.7 -> 1.9 sigma and 15 -> 2.8 sigma respectively), so
  if the RAR constrains the tail anywhere it should be here.
  kernel                        rms bulge-only   rms no-bulge
  framework  sqrt(1+1/y)                0.1061         0.1122
  simple     x/(1+x)                    0.0957         0.1102
  standard   x/sqrt(1+x^2)              0.1119         0.1109
  exponential (McGaugh)                 0.0943         0.1097
  bulge-only kernel spread: 0.0176 dex
  [OK] the bulge subsample separates the kernels by 0.0176 dex, still inside sigma_int -- so the 'RAR wants a gradual transition' half of the standard trade-off is NOT supported by the scatter, at least at fixed a0

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  Framework kernel on the canonical footing: 0.1083 dex, reproducing the committed 0.108
  dex of rar_framework_a0_mlfit.py. Best of any kernel: exponential (McGaugh) at 0.0999 dex.

  1. SPARC IS BLIND TO THE TAIL EXPONENT. Every kernel from alpha = 1 to alpha = infinity
     fits within 0.0084 dex of every other, against an intrinsic scatter of 0.034
     dex. This holds bin by bin and on the bulge subsample. The reason is structural and is
     in S0: almost no SPARC point reaches even y = 10, and none reaches the y ~ 6e7 where the
     Earth orbits. Rotation curves and planetary ephemerides are separated by ~6 orders of
     magnitude in the variable that defines the tail.
  2. SO THE alpha = 1 LIABILITY IS NOT PAID FOR BY GALAXY DATA. The framework is not holding
     alpha = 1 because the rotation curves demand it. It holds alpha = 1 because its
     signature law g_obs^2 = g_bar^2 + a0 g_bar forces it, exactly and at all accelerations.
  3. THAT SHARPENS THE CONFLICT RATHER THAN RESOLVING IT. The available moves are:
     (a) keep the exact law everywhere -> the constant a0/2 anomaly stands and the inner-planet
         bound is exceeded by ~1.3e3 (bare) or ~1.9e2 (after the framework's own EFE);
     (b) declare the law an INFRARED statement that is corrected above some acceleration ->
         passes the planets, costs nothing in SPARC (this script), but the 'exact algebraic
         relation' claim becomes an approximation with an undetermined cutoff, and the
         cutoff is a new free parameter;
     (c) find a modified-inertia mechanism in which the Solar System's high-frequency motion
         is not in the regime the law describes -- the frequency-gate route, which needs a
         fifth constant unless Milgrom 2022's frequency-RATIO construction can supply it.
  4. (b) IS THE HONEST DEFAULT AND SHOULD BE STATED AS SUCH: the framework's exactness claim
     is empirically supported only for y <~ 100, which is where every galaxy datum lives.
     Nothing in the galaxy data is lost by saying so. What is lost is the claim that the
     relation is exact, which is a claim the corpus does make.
  [OK] verdict recorded with the three moves and the default named
====================================================================================================
